MARK MIRCHANDANI: All right.

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:02.032
Just set it down
right over there.

00:00:02.032 --> 00:00:04.789
Yeah.

00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:06.080
ANTHONY BUSHONG: I'm exhausted.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:06.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You said it.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.140
Building all of these
applications is tough work.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:09.950
I don't know what
I'm going to do

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:11.120
when they need to be stateful.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.328
ANTHONY BUSHONG: You know,
Kubernetes on Google Cloud

00:00:13.328 --> 00:00:15.667
Platform is a great way to
host stateful applications

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:16.625
with high availability.

00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:18.095
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That would
save me a ton of headaches.

00:00:18.095 --> 00:00:19.310
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Sounds great.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.310
Let's jump in to a demo
and show you how quickly

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.152
we can get this all set up.

00:00:23.152 --> 00:00:26.128
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.370
Let's start with an
application running

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:33.110
on GKE that requires state.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.660
A simple example would be the
popular blogging platform,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:36.489
WordPress.

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:38.030
So this is our
WordPress architecture

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.100
as represented by
Kubernetes Objects.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.330
We have two key pods--

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.130
the WordPress pod,
which runs a WordPress

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.290
container, and
MariaDB pod, which

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:46.555
runs the backend database.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:47.930
Both of these pods
are controlled

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.010
by deployments and replica
sets which can be scaled up.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.910
Kubernetes services
give us a stable method

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.140
of accessing these pods no
matter the count of replicas.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.090
The MariaDB pod is fronted by an
internal service, type cluster

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.220
IP, while the WordPress
pods are exposed

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.650
to the public via an external
service type load balancer.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.030
This is where we want
to focus our demo today.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.830
Both of these pods
require state.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:13.060
WordPress needs to store assets
in its file system and MariaDB

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.096
stores post and user data.

00:01:15.096 --> 00:01:16.720
So we have a storage
class that defines

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.647
the type of persistent volume
that each pod will get.

00:01:19.647 --> 00:01:21.730
In our case here, we will
want to create a storage

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.349
class for the regional
persistent disk.

00:01:24.349 --> 00:01:26.140
Each pod will then have
a persistent volume

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.126
claim for their respective
persistent volumes.

00:01:29.126 --> 00:01:31.000
Now let's take a look
at how the world worked

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.790
before regional persistent
disks were available.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.310
Let's start with a
single zone GKE cluster.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.920
In this case, we have
GCE persistent disks

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.230
scoped to a single zone
mounted to the nodes

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.560
where our pods are running.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.534
If there's any level of
failure on these nodes,

00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:47.700
we're fortunate to have
Kubernetes Self-Healing kick

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:48.630
in.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:51.270
These pods will migrate to
healthy available nodes,

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.375
and the disk, due to
persistent volume claims,

00:01:53.375 --> 00:01:55.500
will be able to migrate to
the new nodes, retaining

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.650
state for our pods.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.980
All is well.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:01.630
But this isn't everyone's setup.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.520
More and more, we're
looking for reliability

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.380
in our applications,
which means we

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.710
want to spread across
multiple failure domains.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.627
So in GKE, customers are
adopting regional clusters,

00:02:10.627 --> 00:02:12.460
which means that control
plane is replicated

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.520
across multiple zones, as are
the nodes that the applications

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:16.220
run on.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:17.920
In this demo, we will
have nodes running

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.090
in us-west1-b and us-west1-c.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:25.520
With the persistent
disk scoped to a zone,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.340
we'll run into issues to get
the high availability we desire.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:30.840
If there is a
zonal failure, pods

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.180
will continue to migrate
to a different zone

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.470
with healthy nodes.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:37.290
But, because the persistent
disk is scoped to a single zone,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.010
the state cannot follow.

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.710
Enter regional persistent disks.

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.740
In the same scenario, we
can now migrate the state

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.710
to a different zone as it was
being synchronously replicated

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.380
by the regional PDs.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:54.840
Let's jump into the demo
and see it in action.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.070
So here is our regional Google
Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.420
It's running in us-west1,
with nodes in zones b and c.

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.700
Let's use Kube Control
to actually look

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.670
at the pods, deployments,
and service objects

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.100
that comprise our
WordPress application.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:13.700
So as you can see, we have
one of each pod for WordPress

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:16.510
and MariaDB, and we also have
an external load balancer

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.294
fronting the WordPress pod.

00:03:18.294 --> 00:03:20.210
So if we actually throw
that into the browser,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:25.010
we'll see our WordPress blog,
and it's titled, "Hello Take5."

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.010
We'll see that if we dive
into the blog section,

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.487
we actually have one blog post
titled, "Nice Blog, Take5."

00:03:30.487 --> 00:03:32.320
And so we can see that
our state is actually

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:36.460
stored on the persistent
volumes mounted to the MariaDB

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.500
and the WordPress pods.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.520
And we can actually use
Kube Control to look

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.017
at those objects as well.

00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:44.600
So we'll look at the persistent
volumes, the persistent volume

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:47.570
claims, and the specific
storage class that defines

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:51.020
the regional persistent disk.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.470
So we can see that our
claims actually represent

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.140
bounded persistent volumes.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.620
And if we actually dive into
and describe our storage class,

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:02.110
we'll see that we've
created a very specific type

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.171
for regional persistent disk.

00:04:04.171 --> 00:04:05.920
We'll see here, defined
in the parameters,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.000
that we have replication,
type regional persistent disk.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:13.360
We'll see that it's a
persistent disk standard.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:14.800
And it's running
in zones, or it's

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.149
replicated across the zones
us-west1-b and us-west1-c.

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:22.210
So now, we will want to see
the high availability in action

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.420
with regional PDs by
simulating a zonal failure.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:27.640
This script, titled
"Big Red Button,"

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.070
has a command at the end
that will actually take out

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.540
one of the zones
where my nodes are

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:34.980
running for this GKE cluster.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.890
So once I execute that
script, the script

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.010
will actually identify
where both the MariaDB

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.390
and the WordPress
pods are running.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:46.330
So now we see the actual node
that the pod is running on,

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:49.650
and we see that that node
is located in us-west1-b.

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.680
And so we are now going
to simulate that failure

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.090
in us-west1-b, and we will have
to answer yes to that prompt.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.750
And now we will perform a watch
to see what actually happens

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.890
with our pods when they are
moved into the node running

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:06.100
in us-west1-c.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:06.600
OK?

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.500
And now we've actually sped
through the zonal failure

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.120
to see that our pods
have successfully

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.150
rescheduled from the
node in us-west1-b

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.600
to the node in us-west1-c.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.250
Now, to see if state actually
followed into the new zone,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.877
let's jump into
our WordPress blog.

00:05:21.877 --> 00:05:23.460
So if we refresh the
page, we actually

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.860
see that our blog is still
there with the same title.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.330
And let's look at
that blog post.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.580
"Nice Blog, Take5"
is still present,

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.215
and nothing has
changed about it.

00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:31.590
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Great.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.798
So we can see how easy it
is to use Google Kubernetes

00:05:33.798 --> 00:05:36.360
Engine to host stateful,
highly available applications.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.830
ANTHONY BUSHONG: Absolutely.

